<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gotthard Pass</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as St. Gotthard Pass</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gotthard Pass is a 2,106-metre pass in the Swiss <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> linking Uri in the north with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ticino">Ticino</a> in the south. It became a major trade route around 1230, after a bridge was built across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schollenen-gorge">Schöllenen</a> gorge, giving the valley communities of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> strategic value. Railway (1882), road (1980) and base (2016) tunnels later made it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>'s chief north–south Alpine crossing.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gotthard-pass.jpg" alt="Gotthard Pass" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Figugegl · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gotthardpass_2008.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The pass lies on the main watershed of the Gotthard massif, at the lowest point between the summits of Pizzo Lucendro to the west and Pizzo Centrale to the east. Its summit, at 2,106 metres, is in the canton of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ticino">Ticino</a>, about 2 kilometres south of the border with Uri. It joins the valley of the Reuss, called the Urseren, to the valley of the Ticino, called the Valle Leventina, with the towns of Andermatt and Airolo on either side. The saddle is a continental divide between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, which flows to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a>, and the Ticino, which reaches the Po and the Adriatic. The pass was formerly known as Monte Tremolo, and its southern slope is still called Val Tremola.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The pass was known locally in antiquity but was not generally used until the early 13th century, because travellers had to ford the turbulent Reuss in the narrow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schollenen-gorge">Schöllenen Gorge</a> below Andermatt. Once a wooden bridge crossed the gorge, the pass quickly gained in importance. A chapel dedicated to Saint Gotthard of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hildesheim">Hildesheim</a>, patron saint of mountain passes, was built on the southern slope and consecrated by the archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> in 1230, and the pass was known by his name by 1236.</p><p>The opening of the gorge was an important factor in the early Swiss Confederacy. The regions of Uri, Schwyz and Unterwalden gained imperial immediacy under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a> emperors, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pfaffenbrief">Pfaffenbrief</a> of 1370 guaranteed peace along the road from Zürich to the pass. From 1331 the Swiss extended their influence south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> through trade agreements to secure the route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>. In 1403 the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leventina">Leventina</a> became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> of Uri, and a struggle with the Duchy of Milan in the 15th century ended in the Swiss conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ticino">Ticino</a>.</p><p>A new road with the 60-metre Urnerloch tunnel, the first road tunnel in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>, was built in 1707/8. Only foot traffic and pack animals crossed until 1775, when the first carriage made the journey, and the Battle of Gotthard Pass took place on 24 September 1799.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>A road wide enough for single-lane motorised traffic was built in 1830, and the first car is said to have crossed the pass in 1895. Three tunnels now carry most of the traffic beneath the massif, each the world's longest of its kind when built: the 15-kilometre Gotthard Rail Tunnel of 1882, which cost about 200 workers' lives, the 17-kilometre Gotthard Road Tunnel of 1980, and the 57-kilometre Gotthard Base Tunnel of 2016, which gives a nearly level railway route from Erstfeld to Bodio. With the Lötschberg it is one of the two main north-south routes through the Swiss Alps. Near the summit are several lakes, a hospice and a museum.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gotthard Pass” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The opening of a route over the <b>Gotthard Pass</b> in the early thirteenth century, made possible by bridges through the Schöllenen gorge, gave the valleys of Uri, Schwyz and Unterwalden new strategic and commercial value, and Uri in 1231 and Schwyz in 1240 obtained charters placing them directly…</blockquote><a href="/empire/old-swiss-confederacy">Old Swiss Confederacy · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Lucerne grew around the monastery of Saint Leodegar where the Reuss leaves Lake Lucerne, and boomed after the opening of the <b>Gotthard</b> route in the 13th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/canton-of-lucerne">Canton of Lucerne</a></li><li><blockquote>When the <b>Gotthard Pass</b> was opened to traffic in the early 13th century, the valley gained strategic value, and in 1231 King Henry VII bought it back from the Habsburgs and granted it imperial immediacy.</blockquote><a href="/state/canton-of-uri">Canton of Uri</a></li></ul></section>
